    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay reveals that for more than a century, the visual imagination of space in the United States has been entangled with the specific technologies through which we viewed and eventually traveled to it and with national political priorities. The author analyzes the rhetoric at play in a range of materials including the stories of Jules Verne, pulp fiction magazines of the late 1920s, Star Trek, 2001: A Space Odyssey, and Star Wars, showing the specific fantasies and pragmatic goals at work within them.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
